--TEST--
`Core\Secret::reveal` removes `secret` and carries `tainted` through, so a revealed value declared as a plain `string` is refused — `rule:core-classes/secret-reveal`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The two qualifiers are independent bits (`rule:security/secret-qualifier`), and § 3's escape
// hatch is on one of them: revealing says the caller may see the value, not
// that the value came from somewhere trusted. A `Qual::Reveal` that dropped
// both would launder `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`'s axis for free at the one call site written to
// disclose — the leak-shaped failure, and it compiles, which is why this case
// asserts the refusal rather than the acceptance.
secret tainted string $both = "t";

// The accepted spelling first, so a reveal that stopped carrying the qualifier
// through fails here as a missing refusal below rather than looking correct.
tainted string $ok = Core\Secret::reveal($both, "the audit log records which command disclosed it");
echo $ok, "\n";

// And the refusal: the answer is `tainted string`, so the binding that claims
// otherwise is an ordinary `E0401`. No rule of its own — the qualifier is part
// of the type, which is the whole point of `rule:security/secret-qualifier`'s representation.
string $laundered = Core\Secret::reveal($both, "the audit log records which command disclosed it");
echo $laundered, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:18:21
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
